WEBVTT

00:03.920 --> 00:07.400
Now the replace function can go and replace a specific character.

00:07.400 --> 00:11.000
So that means we have something old and we want to replace it with something new.

00:11.040 --> 00:12.920
Let's have a very simple example to understand it.

00:12.960 --> 00:13.320
All right.

00:13.320 --> 00:17.120
So now imagine we have a phone number where the data is splitted by a dash.

00:17.160 --> 00:19.960
Now let's say that I don't like to have the dash in my data.

00:20.000 --> 00:22.800
I would like to have slash like any other special character.

00:22.840 --> 00:26.440
Now, in order to replace the dash, we can use the function replace.

00:26.440 --> 00:29.080
So we have to specify for SQL two things.

00:29.080 --> 00:32.800
The old value the dash with a new value the slash.

00:32.800 --> 00:37.600
So if you do that in the output it's going to go and remove all those dashes between the numbers.

00:37.600 --> 00:40.280
And the replacement is going to be the dash between them.

00:40.280 --> 00:41.760
So it's very simple right.

00:41.760 --> 00:45.160
All what you are doing is replacing an old value with a new value.

00:45.160 --> 00:46.840
And that's why we call it replace.

00:46.840 --> 00:50.400
But we can use this function as well in order to remove something.

00:50.440 --> 00:51.840
Not only we replace.

00:51.840 --> 00:57.080
And you can do that by not specifying anything in the new value like just the single quotes.

00:57.080 --> 00:59.200
And with that it's going to be nothing a blank.

00:59.200 --> 01:03.600
So now what's going to happen is clearly going to go and replace the dash with a blank.

01:03.640 --> 01:07.120
And that means I'm just removing the dashes from the output.

01:07.120 --> 01:10.280
So if you do it you will remove the dash and you will get only numbers.

01:10.280 --> 01:16.000
So if the replacement is going to be a blank, then that means this function will be replacing any value

01:16.000 --> 01:16.960
that you specify.

01:17.000 --> 01:18.640
So this is exactly how it works.

01:18.640 --> 01:21.560
And this is why we use the replace function in SQL.

01:21.600 --> 01:23.480
Now let's go back in order to practice.

01:23.520 --> 01:24.920
So let's do the same example.

01:24.920 --> 01:27.520
This time we're going to go and select from static value.

01:27.520 --> 01:31.800
So we're going to get 1234567890.

01:31.840 --> 01:35.320
So if we go and execute it you can see we are getting the phone number.

01:35.360 --> 01:37.480
Now let's go and remove the dashes from this value.

01:37.480 --> 01:38.680
So let's have a new line.

01:38.680 --> 01:40.560
And we start with replace.

01:40.640 --> 01:44.120
The first thing that you have to specify for SQL the value itself.

01:44.120 --> 01:45.880
So let's go and get the value.

01:45.920 --> 01:47.240
This is the first argument.

01:47.280 --> 01:49.640
The second argument is going to be the old value.

01:49.640 --> 01:51.560
So the old value is going to be the dash.

01:51.600 --> 01:54.920
And now the third argument will be the replacement.

01:54.920 --> 01:58.600
And since we want to remove it we don't want to replace it with anything.

01:58.600 --> 02:01.240
We will have just single quotes and nothing between them.

02:01.240 --> 02:03.840
So there is no space between those single quotes.

02:03.880 --> 02:09.040
Now we can go and rename stuff like this is the phone and this is a clean phone.

02:09.480 --> 02:11.000
Let's go and execute it.

02:11.040 --> 02:16.000
Now as you can see in the output of the function, we don't have any dashes between the numbers.

02:16.000 --> 02:21.370
And you can go and test stuff like for example I can go and add slash and execute it.

02:21.370 --> 02:23.050
You will see slashes between them.

02:23.050 --> 02:24.890
So you can go and try multiple stuff.

02:24.890 --> 02:27.610
So this is one nice use case for the replace function.

02:27.610 --> 02:33.530
Now there is another use case for the replace function is that sometimes in my data file names can be

02:33.530 --> 02:37.010
stored like for example let's say reports dot txt.

02:37.050 --> 02:42.210
And now let's say that I would like to change the file format from txt to CSV.

02:42.250 --> 02:43.330
Now how are we going to do that.

02:43.330 --> 02:45.930
We're going to go with a new line say replace.

02:45.930 --> 02:48.250
And then the first argument is going to be the value.

02:48.250 --> 02:50.410
So let's take our value from here.

02:50.410 --> 02:52.010
And now what is the old value.

02:52.050 --> 02:53.850
It's going to be the dot txt.

02:54.090 --> 02:58.290
And I want to replace it with another format with another extension.

02:58.290 --> 02:59.650
So it's going to be the CSV.

02:59.690 --> 03:02.450
So we're going to say this is the new file name.

03:02.770 --> 03:05.810
And this is the old file name.

03:05.810 --> 03:07.410
So let's go and execute it.

03:07.410 --> 03:12.450
And now as you can see in the output SQL did replace the txt with a CSV.

03:12.490 --> 03:15.970
This as well where I use the replace function in my projects.

03:15.970 --> 03:18.290
So my friends the replace function is really fun.

03:18.290 --> 03:20.890
And those are two nice use cases for the replace.

03:20.930 --> 03:21.290
All right.

03:21.290 --> 03:23.450
So that's all about the replace function in SQL.

03:23.450 --> 03:26.210
And with that we have covered the whole data manipulations.

